{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · Paradoxes as vantages","loop":56,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.799411765,-0.108529412,-0.492352941,0.05202]},{"t":0.33,"box":[-0.799411765,-0.108529412,-0.492352941,0.05202]},{"t":0.36,"box":[-0.428347059,-0.112174412,-0.214888235,0.0577588235]},{"t":0.5,"box":[-0.428347059,-0.112174412,-0.214888235,0.0577588235]},{"t":0.53,"box":[-0.160941176,-0.0688182353,0.182117647,0.009]},{"t":0.67,"box":[-0.160941176,-0.0688182353,0.182117647,0.009]},{"t":0.7,"box":[0.236302941,-0.110461765,0.471785294,0.0392638232]},{"t":0.84,"box":[0.236302941,-0.110461765,0.471785294,0.0392638232]},{"t":0.87,"box":[0.507811765,-0.108952941,0.821011765,0.0257294053]}],"stops":[0.16,0.33,0.5,0.67,0.84],"beats":[{"title":"Each sees the other smaller","text":"Two people stand at opposite ends of a field. [A](#lit:1,2) reads [B](#lit:5) against [its own hand at arm's length](#lit:7), its thumb; [B](#lit:3,4) reads [A](#lit:6) against [its own](#lit:8). Each sees the other smaller than its hand, and both are right.\n\nThe contradiction comes when [the two readings](#lit:10,11) are written on [one number line](#lit:9), as if measured against one shared length. No such length was agreed: each denominator is private to its seat."},{"title":"The exchange","text":"Each reading holds the same distance factor and the size ratio, one way up in each. So [A tells B its reading, and B tells A](#lit:12).\n\nThe square root of their ratio is the sizes' ratio, [½](#lit:10): who is bigger, with no ruler. The square root of their product is the distance in arm's lengths, [¼](#lit:11). Neither seat alone holds either answer; both are bought at the price of one exchange, and nothing is written."},{"title":"Simpson's paradox","text":"Kidney stones, treatment A against B (Charig et al. 1986). A succeeded more often for [small stones](#lit:15,16), 93% against 87%, and for [large stones](#lit:17,18), 73% against 69%. [Pooled](#lit:19,20), A succeeded 78% of the time and B 83%.\n\nA success rate is a ratio over a group, and [the groups differ](#lit:21,22,23,24): three in four of A's patients had large stones, about one in four of B's. Bought against one standard mix, A is ahead again, 83% against 78%."},{"title":"Finite by length, endless by count","text":"To cross a distance, first cross half of it, then half of what is left, without end. The writer holds the whole series and sums it to one: drawn by length, [one thumb to two, two to four, four to eight](#lit:29) all fit inside [one more thumb](#lit:27).\n\nThe reader counts the steps, and each takes the same share of its fineness, so the count never ends. Finite by length and endless by count, both true, from different vantages."},{"title":"No scale preferred","text":"In many real collections of numbers the leading digit is 1 about 30% of the time. [An even spread of digits](#lit:31,32,33,34,35,36,37,38,39), 11% each, assumes a writer's scale. Met with no scale preferred, every stretch from an amount to twice it gets the same room, and [the ruler](#lit:40) lays its digits by their ratios.\n\nEach cell is then the digit's share, log₁₀(1 + 1/d): 30.1% for a 1, 4.6% for a 9 (Hill 1995). The two envelopes, the St Petersburg game and Weber and Fechner are the same puzzle."},{"title":"Read or written?","text":"One test sorts the catalogue. Scale the world: [everything in the field](#lit:43,44,45,46) 1.8 times as large. [A's view of B](#lit:42) is the same angle, and [the readings](#lit:49,50) do not move: they are readings. Against [a metre](#lit:47), every size moved: \"who is bigger, in metres\" is a writing, and needed something carried in.\n\nA paradox, nearly always, is the two run together. Nothing here is a new resolution; the assumption has one form, and the test finds it. [The essay](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/09-paradoxes-as-vantages.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.251470588,0.251470588,-0.251470588,-0.251470588],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":6},{"morph":5},{"morph":1},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":12},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":26},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":30},{"morph":31},{"morph":32},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":35},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":44},{"morph":45},{"morph":46},{"morph":47},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":59},{"morph":60},{"morph":61},{"morph":88},{"morph":62},{"morph":89},{"morph":63},{"morph":90},{"morph":91},{"morph":92},{"morph":93},{"morph":64},{"morph":94},{"morph":95},{"morph":96},{"morph":53},{"morph":77},{"morph":78},{"morph":79},{"morph":80},{"morph":54},{"morph":81},{"morph":82},{"morph":83},{"morph":84},{"morph":55},{"morph":85},{"morph":86},{"morph":56},{"morph":57},{"morph":65},{"morph":58},{"morph":87},{"morph":66},{"morph":67},{"morph":68},{"morph":69},{"morph":70},{"morph":71},{"morph":72},{"morph":73},{"morph":74},{"morph":75},{"morph":97},{"morph":98},{"morph":99},{"morph":100},{"morph":76},{"morph":101}],"morphs":[{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":null,"keys":[{"x":[-0.778235294,-0.513529412],"y":[-0.0397058824,-0.0397058824],"r":[0,0],"c":[null,null]}],"fill":null,"inPlace":true,"lag":0,"fx":null,"own":{"parent":-1,"parentAt":null,"follow":null,"look":-1,"lookRef":0,"phase":0,"rate":1,"spin":0,"motion":null,"onto":null,"laps":1,"start":null,"times":null,"clip":{"start":0.004,"len":0.986,"show":"","in":{"kind":"draw","len":0.01}},"eases":null,"straight":false,"trail":null,"cols":null,"path":n